edit("Abstract:", whole="Abstract: Resting-state fMRI turns every scan into a brain network, but very few labelled scans exist. This study introduces a model that tracks small brain subgraphs over time and learns normal behavior from control scans only; unusual regions receive a high anomaly score. Real control scans then serve to generate structure-aware anomalies by gradient-guided edge-weight edits. Guided edits get detected far better than random edits: on 70 held-out control scans the AUROC is 0.906 against 0.491, and GIN + GRU detects them best among four encoder and predictor pairs. In few-shot adaptation, generated anomalies lift one-shot F1 from 0.006 to about 0.40, while AUROC stays near 0.5 because more patients get flagged without a better ranking. Direct ASD detection on ABIDE I stays at chance level (AUROC 0.453) and severity bands do not beat a majority-class guess; both stay as limits of the present data and features.")
edit("The brain activity measures", (" BrainGNN [8], BrainGB [9] and Brain Network Transformer [10] analyze the networks of the subjects separately.", ""))
