edit("To determine the detectability", whole="To test the detectability of generated anomalies, the 70 held-out test control scans were used. For each scan and each of the 20 regions, one window transition received a guided edit and one a random edit of the same magnitude at severity 0.9. The unedited transition is the negative example and the edited one the positive example (1,400 pairs per method); a transition counts as detected when its score exceeds the validation cut-off of 7.43. All settings were fixed beforehand, the checkpoint with the best validation loss (epoch 4 of 5) was used and test data served only for the final evaluation. Uncertainty is a 95% bootstrap interval (2,000 resamples) with a permutation test (5,000 shuffles); few-shot results give the mean and standard deviation over five draws of ASD scans, and the chance F1 for flag rate q is 2πq/(π + q), with π = 0.466 the share of ASD patients in the test set.")


edit("Table III presents", whole="Guided sub-structure edits get detected far better than random edits of the same size on every metric (Table III): accuracy 70.2% versus 50.0%, precision 0.902 versus 0.500, recall 0.454 versus 0.049, F1 0.604 versus 0.090 and AUROC 0.906 versus 0.491. Random edits stay at chance level, while guided editing raised the mean transition score from 2.94 to 7.62. Because the model that decides the edits also scores them, this test shows the visibility of the generated anomalies and not an ASD diagnosis.")
hp = find("G. Identification of produced anomalies")
for r in hp.runs:
    if "Identification of produced anomalies" in r.text: r.text = r.text.replace("Identification of produced anomalies", "Guided sub-structure versus random edits"); break
else: raise SystemExit("heading run")


